---
import SEO from "../components/SEO.astro";
---

<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />

  <meta
    name="description"
    content="Help Barkly Labs build its local AI infrastructure by donating hardware, computing equipment, electronics, and other useful technology."
  />

  <meta
    name="twitter:description"
    content="Donate hardware to Barkly Labs and help build the infrastructure behind CYN-X."
  />

  <meta
    property="og:description"
    content="Help Barkly Labs build CYN-X and future human-centered technology through hardware donations."
  />

  <meta
    property="og:title"
    content="Build the Node — Hardware Donations — Barkly Labs"
  />

  <meta
    property="og:url"
    content="https://www.barklylabs.space/hardware/"
  />

  <link
    rel="canonical"
    href="https://www.barklylabs.space/hardware/"
  />

  <meta name="robots" content="index, follow" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />

  <title>Build the Node — Hardware Donations — Barkly Labs</title>

  <SEO />
</head>

<body>
  <header class="site-header">
    <div class="header-logo">
      <a href={import.meta.env.BASE_URL + ""}>
        <span>BARKLY</span> <strong>LABS</strong>
      </a>
    </div>

    <div class="header-document">
      <span>HARDWARE</span>
      <span>/</span>
      <span>DOCUMENT 001</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main>
    <aside class="side-nav">
      <a href="#mission">01 MISSION</a>
      <a href="#node">02 THE NODE</a>
      <a href="#hardware">03 HARDWARE</a>
      <a href="#give">04 GIVE HARDWARE</a>
      <a href="#reuse">05 SECOND LIFE</a>
      <a href="#transparency">06 TRANSPARENCY</a>
    </aside>

    <!-- HERO -->

    <section class="hero-section" id="mission">
      <div class="section-number">01 / MISSION</div>

      <div class="hero-content">
        <div class="eyebrow">
          BARKLY LABS / HARDWARE DONATION PROGRAM
        </div>

        <h1>
          BUILD<br />
          <span>THE NODE.</span>
        </h1>

        <p class="hero-copy">
          Barkly Labs is building dedicated computing infrastructure for
          <strong>CYN-X</strong>, our local AI platform and experimental
          technology stack.
        </p>

        <p class="hero-copy muted">
          We don't need every piece of hardware to be new. We need useful
          hardware to have somewhere useful to go.
        </p>

        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="primary-button" href="#hardware">
            SEE WHAT WE NEED →
          </a>

          <a class="secondary-button" href="#give">
            DONATE HARDWARE →
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- WHY -->

    <section class="content-section" id="node">
      <div class="section-number">02 / THE NODE</div>

      <div class="section-heading">
        <h2>
          COMPUTE<br />
          <span>SHOULD BE INFRASTRUCTURE.</span>
        </h2>

        <p>
          CYN-X needs somewhere to run. Instead of making every development
          machine carry the entire workload, Barkly is building a dedicated
          local compute node that can serve the laboratory.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="content-grid">
        <div class="content-block">
          <div class="block-label">01 / LOCAL AI</div>

          <h3>RUN THE WORK LOCALLY.</h3>

          <p>
            The compute node will provide dedicated hardware for local AI
            inference, experimentation, model testing, and CYN-X development
            without requiring every developer machine to carry the workload.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="content-block">
          <div class="block-label">02 / DEVELOPMENT</div>

          <h3>MAKE DEVELOPMENT POSSIBLE.</h3>

          <p>
            Dedicated infrastructure means Barkly can keep development tools,
            services, models, APIs, documentation systems, and experiments
            running without turning a personal laptop into the entire lab.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="content-block">
          <div class="block-label">03 / COMMUNITY</div>

          <h3>BUILD SOMETHING OTHER PEOPLE CAN USE.</h3>

          <p>
            Infrastructure built for one project can become infrastructure
            for many. The node can support future Barkly projects, experiments,
            educational material, documentation, and community technology.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="content-block">
          <div class="block-label">04 / OPEN WORK</div>

          <h3>LET THE HARDWARE BECOME KNOWLEDGE.</h3>

          <p>
            The goal isn't simply to own a powerful computer. It's to use the
            machine to create software, documentation, experiments, and
            knowledge that can be shared with other humans.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="human-note">
        <span class="note-mark">♡</span>

        <div>
          <strong>
            THE COMPUTER IS A TOOL. THE HUMAN IS THE POINT.
          </strong>

          <p>
            We're building infrastructure so people can spend more time
            creating and less time fighting their hardware.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- HARDWARE -->

    <section class="content-section hardware-section" id="hardware">
      <div class="section-number">03 / HARDWARE</div>

      <div class="section-heading">
        <h2>
          WHAT CAN<br />
          <span>YOU GIVE?</span>
        </h2>

        <p>
          We're interested in useful computing hardware in many forms:
          retired enterprise equipment, surplus components, workstation parts,
          complete systems, and things sitting unused in storage.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="hardware-grid">

        <article class="hardware-card featured">
          <span class="hardware-index">01</span>

          <div class="hardware-tag">HIGH PRIORITY</div>

          <h3>AI GPUs</h3>

          <p>
            NVIDIA CUDA-capable GPUs and other accelerators with substantial
            VRAM are especially useful for CYN-X and local AI development.
          </p>

          <div class="hardware-examples">
            P40 / P4 / RTX / RTX A-SERIES / A4000 / A5000 / A6000
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="hardware-card">
          <span class="hardware-index">02</span>

          <div class="hardware-tag">HIGH PRIORITY</div>

          <h3>SERVER & WORKSTATION COMPUTERS</h3>

          <p>
            Complete servers, workstations, tower systems, and enterprise
            computers can become the foundation of the compute node.
          </p>

          <div class="hardware-examples">
            XEON / EPYC / THREADRIPPER / WORKSTATION SYSTEMS
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="hardware-card">
          <span class="hardware-index">03</span>

          <div class="hardware-tag">HIGH PRIORITY</div>

          <h3>ECC MEMORY</h3>

          <p>
            Server and workstation memory is extremely useful for building
            reliable local infrastructure with enough capacity for AI
            workloads and development services.
          </p>

          <div class="hardware-examples">
            DDR3 ECC / DDR4 ECC / DDR5 ECC / RDIMM / UDIMM
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="hardware-card">
          <span class="hardware-index">04</span>

          <div class="hardware-tag">USEFUL</div>

          <h3>STORAGE</h3>

          <p>
            SATA SSDs, NVMe drives, enterprise SSDs, hard drives, and storage
            controllers can provide space for operating systems, models,
            datasets, documentation, and backups.
          </p>

          <div class="hardware-examples">
            SATA SSD / NVME / HDD / ENTERPRISE STORAGE
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="hardware-card">
          <span class="hardware-index">05</span>

          <div class="hardware-tag">USEFUL</div>

          <h3>POWER & COOLING</h3>

          <p>
            Power supplies, CPU coolers, server fans, case fans, thermal
            hardware, cables, and other infrastructure can make donated
            components usable.
          </p>

          <div class="hardware-examples">
            PSU / COOLERS / FANS / CABLES / POWER HARDWARE
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="hardware-card">
          <span class="hardware-index">06</span>

          <div class="hardware-tag">ALWAYS USEFUL</div>

          <h3>OTHER TECHNOLOGY</h3>

          <p>
            Networking equipment, monitors, development boards, electronics,
            adapters, test equipment, and other useful technology may also
            have a place in the lab.
          </p>

          <div class="hardware-examples">
            SWITCHES / ROUTERS / BOARDS / MONITORS / TEST EQUIPMENT
          </div>
        </article>

      </div>

      <div class="hardware-callout">
        <div class="callout-symbol">+</div>

        <div>
          <strong>
            DON'T KNOW IF IT'S USEFUL?
          </strong>

          <p>
            Ask us anyway. We would rather look at a piece of hardware and
            determine whether it has a useful role than have something useful
            end up in a landfill.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- GIVE -->

    <section class="content-section give-section" id="give">
      <div class="section-number">04 / GIVE HARDWARE</div>

      <div class="section-heading">
        <h2>
          GIVE THE LAB<br />
          <span>A PIECE OF IT.</span>
        </h2>

        <p>
          Hardware donations don't have to be complicated. A company retiring
          equipment, an engineer with spare components, or a person with an
          unused workstation can all help build the infrastructure.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="support-options">

        <article class="support-card featured">
          <div class="card-top">
            <span>01</span>
            <span>COMPLETE SYSTEM</span>
          </div>

          <h3>DONATE A COMPUTER</h3>

          <p>
            Have a workstation, server, or powerful desktop that isn't being
            used anymore? It may be able to become part of Barkly's compute
            infrastructure.
          </p>

          <div class="card-bottom">
            <span>SYSTEM → NODE</span>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="support-card">
          <div class="card-top">
            <span>02</span>
            <span>COMPONENT</span>
          </div>

          <h3>DONATE PARTS</h3>

          <p>
            GPUs, CPUs, memory, storage, power supplies, cooling hardware,
            motherboards, networking equipment, and other components can be
            combined into working systems.
          </p>

          <div class="card-bottom">
            <span>PART → INFRASTRUCTURE</span>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="support-card">
          <div class="card-top">
            <span>03</span>
            <span>BUSINESS</span>
          </div>

          <h3>RETIRING IT EQUIPMENT?</h3>

          <p>
            If your organization is replacing computers, servers, networking
            equipment, or other technology, consider giving Barkly the
            equipment that still has useful life.
          </p>

          <div class="card-bottom">
            <span>SURPLUS → SECOND LIFE</span>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="support-card">
          <div class="card-top">
            <span>04</span>
            <span>PARTNER</span>
          </div>

          <h3>HELP US SOURCE IT</h3>

          <p>
            You don't need to personally own hardware. Introductions to
            companies, IT departments, refurbishers, recyclers, universities,
            makerspaces, and other equipment holders are valuable too.
          </p>

          <div class="card-bottom">
            <span>CONNECTION → CAPACITY</span>
          </div>
        </article>

      </div>

      <div class="donation-panel">
        <div class="donation-panel-header">
          <span>HARDWARE DONATIONS / 001</span>
          <span>BUILD THE NODE</span>
        </div>

        <div class="donation-panel-content">

          <div>
            <h3>
              HAVE<br />
              <span>HARDWARE?</span>
            </h3>

            <p>
              Tell Barkly Labs what you have, where it is, and what you know
              about it. We can determine whether it fits the current compute
              node or another laboratory project.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="contact-container">
            <span>START A HARDWARE DONATION</span>

            <strong>
              LET'S SEE WHAT<br />
              YOU'VE GOT.
            </strong>

            <a class="primary-button" href="/contact">
              CONTACT BARKLY →
            </a>

            <small>
              Please include the manufacturer, model, approximate condition,
              quantity, and location when possible.
            </small>
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- SECOND LIFE -->

    <section class="content-section reuse-section" id="reuse">
      <div class="section-number">05 / SECOND LIFE</div>

      <div class="section-heading">
        <h2>
          RETIRED<br />
          <span>DOESN'T MEAN USELESS.</span>
        </h2>

        <p>
          Technology is replaced constantly. That doesn't mean every replaced
          machine has finished doing useful work.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="reuse-grid">

        <div class="reuse-item">
          <span>01</span>

          <div>
            <strong>EXTEND THE LIFE OF HARDWARE</strong>

            <p>
              A server that is no longer appropriate for a corporate workload
              may still be excellent infrastructure for a small laboratory.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="reuse-item">
          <span>02</span>

          <div>
            <strong>KEEP USEFUL COMPUTE OUT OF WASTE STREAMS</strong>

            <p>
              When equipment can be reused safely and responsibly, its useful
              life doesn't have to end when an organization upgrades.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="reuse-item">
          <span>03</span>

          <div>
            <strong>TURN HARDWARE INTO OPEN WORK</strong>

            <p>
              Donated hardware can become software, documentation,
              experiments, research infrastructure, and public knowledge.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="reuse-item">
          <span>04</span>

          <div>
            <strong>MAKE INFRASTRUCTURE ACCESSIBLE</strong>

            <p>
              Not everyone can afford dedicated AI hardware. Shared laboratory
              infrastructure can lower the barrier to experimentation.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="reuse-principle">
        <div class="reuse-principle-mark">+</div>

        <div>
          <span>THE BARKLY PRINCIPLE</span>

          <strong>
            BUILD WITH<br />
            WHAT ALREADY EXISTS.
          </strong>

          <p>
            Good engineering isn't always about buying the newest thing.
            Sometimes it's about looking at what humans already have and
            figuring out what it can become.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- TRANSPARENCY -->

    <section class="content-section transparency-section" id="transparency">
      <div class="section-number">06 / TRANSPARENCY</div>

      <div class="section-heading">
        <h2>
          SHOW THE<br />
          <span>HARDWARE.</span>
        </h2>

        <p>
          If someone gives Barkly a piece of equipment, they should be able to
          understand where it went and what it helped make possible.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="transparency-grid">

        <div class="transparency-item">
          <span>01</span>

          <strong>DONATION RECORD</strong>

          <p>
            We intend to maintain records of donated equipment, including
            what was received and when it entered the lab.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="transparency-item">
          <span>02</span>

          <strong>HARDWARE INVENTORY</strong>

          <p>
            Equipment can be tracked as part of the laboratory's physical
            infrastructure rather than disappearing into an undocumented pile
            of parts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="transparency-item">
          <span>03</span>

          <strong>WHAT IT BECAME</strong>

          <p>
            Whenever practical, we want to show what a donated component
            ultimately became: a server, workstation, experiment, prototype,
            or another useful system.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="transparency-item">
          <span>04</span>

          <strong>PUBLIC WORK</strong>

          <p>
            The goal is for donated hardware to create something visible:
            software, documentation, experiments, research, or infrastructure
            that can help other humans.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="final-statement">
        <div class="statement-line"></div>

        <p>
          HARDWARE IS ONLY<br />
          <span>THE BEGINNING.</span>
        </p>

        <div class="statement-line"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CONTACT -->

    <section class="contact-section" id="contact">
      <div class="contact-inner">
        <div class="section-number">CONTACT / HARDWARE 001</div>

        <h2>
          HAVE<br />
          <span>SOMETHING?</span>
        </h2>

        <p>
          Whether you're an individual with spare parts, an engineer with an
          old workstation, an organization retiring servers, or someone who
          knows where useful hardware is sitting — we'd love to hear from you.
        </p>

        <a class="primary-button large-button" href="/contact">
          CONTACT BARKLY →
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer class="site-footer">
    <div>
      <strong>BARKLY LABS</strong>
      <span>HUMAN FIRST</span>
    </div>

    <div>
      HARDWARE / DOCUMENT 001
    </div>
  </footer>
</body>

<style>
  :global(html) {
    scroll-behavior: smooth;
  }

  :global(body) {
    margin: 0;
    background: #050505;
    color: #f1f1f1;
    font-family:
      "Courier New",
      Courier,
      monospace;
  }

  :global(*) {
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }

  :global(a) {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .site-header {
    position: sticky;
    top: 0;
    z-index: 100;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 22px 38px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #222;
    background: rgba(5, 5, 5, 0.94);
    backdrop-filter: blur(12px);
  }

  .header-logo {
    font-size: 20px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }

  .header-logo span {
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .header-logo strong {
    color: #ff2f92;
    font-weight: 400;
  }

  .header-document {
    display: flex;
    gap: 10px;
    color: #686868;
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
  }

  .header-document span:first-child {
    color: #ff2f92;
  }

  .side-nav {
    position: fixed;
    left: 38px;
    top: 50%;
    z-index: 20;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 10px;
    transform: translateY(-50%);
  }

  .side-nav a {
    color: #686868;
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    transition: color 0.2s ease;
  }

  .side-nav a:hover {
    color: #ff2f92;
  }

  .hero-section,
  .content-section {
    width: min(1200px, calc(100% - 180px));
    margin-left: auto;
    margin-right: auto;
  }

  .hero-section {
    min-height: 90vh;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: 100px 0;
  }

  .section-number {
    margin-bottom: 34px;
    color: #ff2f92;
    font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    margin-bottom: 28px;
    color: #686868;
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
  }

  h1,
  h2,
  h3,
  p {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  h1 {
    margin-bottom: 38px;
    font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
    font-size: clamp(64px, 10vw, 150px);
    line-height: 0.86;
    letter-spacing: -0.075em;
  }

  h1 span,
  h2 span,
  .reuse-principle strong,
  .final-statement span {
    color: #ff2f92;
  }

  .hero-copy {
    max-width: 760px;
    margin-bottom: 18px;
    font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
    font-size: 22px;
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: #f1f1f1;
  }

  .hero-copy.muted {
    max-width: 680px;
    color: #a1a1a1;
    font-size: 16px;
  }

  .hero-actions {
    display: flex;
    gap: 12px;
    margin-top: 30px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  .primary-button,
  .secondary-button {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    min-height: 48px;
    padding: 0 22px;
    border: 1px solid #ff2f92;
    font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    transition:
      background 0.2s ease,
      color 0.2s ease,
      transform 0.2s ease;
  }

  .primary-button {
    background: #ff2f92;
    color: #050505;
  }

  .secondary-button {
    color: #ff2f92;
  }

  .primary-button:hover,
  .secondary-button:hover {
    transform: translateY(-2px);
  }

  .secondary-button:hover {
    background: #ff2f92;
    color: #050505;
  }

  .content-section {
    padding: 130px 0;
    border-top: 1px solid #222;
  }

  .section-heading {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.2fr) minmax(280px, 0.8fr);
    gap: 80px;
    align-items: end;
    margin-bottom: 70px;
  }

  .section-heading h2 {
    margin-bottom: 0;
    font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
    font-size: clamp(50px, 7vw, 100px);
    line-height: 0.9;
    letter-spacing: -0.065em;
  }

  .section-heading p {
    margin-bottom: 4px;
    color: #a1a1a1;
    font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .content-grid,
  .hardware-grid,
  .support-options,
  .reuse-grid,
  .transparency-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: 1px;
    background: #222;
    border: 1px solid #222;
  }

  .content-block,
  .hardware-card,
  .support-card,
  .reuse-item,
  .transparency-item {
    padding: 34px;
    background: #050505;
  }

  .content-block {
    min-height: 280px;
  }

  .block-label,
  .hardware-tag {
    margin-bottom: 50px;
    color: #ff2f92;
    font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
  }

  .content-block h3,
  .hardware-card h3,
  .support-card h3 {
    margin-bottom: 18px;
    font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
    font-size: 25px;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
  }

  .content-block p,
  .hardware-card p,
  .support-card p,
  .reuse-item p,
  .transparency-item p {
    max-width: 520px;
    color: #a1a1a1;
    font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .human-note {
    display: flex;
    gap: 24px;
    align-items: flex-start;
    margin-top: 34px;
    padding: 30px;
    border: 1px solid #333;
    background:
      linear-gradient(
        135deg,
        rgba(255, 47, 146, 0.08),
        transparent 55%
      );
  }

  .note-mark,
  .callout-symbol {
    color: #ff2f92;
    font-size: 30px;
  }

  .human-note strong,
  .hardware-callout strong {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
    font-size: 12px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }

  .human-note p {
    margin: 0;
    color: #686868;
    font-size: 11px;
  }

  .hardware-card {
    min-height: 300px;
  }

  .hardware-card.featured {
    background:
      linear-gradient(
        145deg,
        rgba(255, 47, 146, 0.12),
        #050505 65%
      );
  }

  .hardware-index {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 18px;
    color: #ff2f92;
